#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060463} :activity/id #uuid "a1eb1fd3-aa6a-56be-bbd3-db712b716efe" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049534 :topic/id #uuid "fca2fb04-ac83-5ee2-aa8c-69f7742f701b" :topic/math-academy-id 165} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060463 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060870 :tutorial/content "A **piecewise function** is a function that is defined differently in different parts of the function's domain.\n\nFor example, consider the piecewise function $f(x)$, defined as follows:\n\n$$\n{-2x,\\begin{vmatrix}x \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x,\\end{vmatrix}x > 0\n$$\n\nA plot of the function is given below:\n\n![](images/11/111157ad82daacd5cbca8095c9fa1645ea9920e9bb9b26e8588e23dc492bca0d.png)\n\nThe function has two distinct branches, one for\n\n$$\n\\color{red}{x\\leq 0}\n$$\n\nand one for $\\color{blue}{x>0}$. The function is a piecewise function because it has different definitions depending on where we are in the domain.\n\nSuppose that we want to calculate $f(-1)$. For this, we'd use the left branch\n\n$$\nx \\leq 0\n$$\n\nbecause\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq 0\n$$\n\nOn this branch, the function definition is\n\n$$\nf(x) = -2x\n$$\n\nso we have\n\n$$\nf(-1) = -2(-1) = 2\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if we want to calculate $f(1)$, we'd use the right branch $x > 0$ because $1 > 0$. On this branch, the function definition is\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nso we have\n\n$$\nf(1) = \\dfrac 1 2 \\cdot 1 = \\dfrac 1 2\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "78de19f4-277e-593e-b417-d9c4b49d919f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1823 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "271b7b22-3952-5a9c-b297-a12548098709" :step/math-academy-id 104209 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060464}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060464 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059632 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059458 :question/id #uuid "92d4072d-90d5-5b12-b043-9361efb446cd" :question/math-academy-id "e-1749"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d